= Replication — Technical =

== Study-level estimands ==
A replication should clarify whether it targets the same:

* estimand;
* theoretical construct;
* operationalization;
* population;
* intervention;
* outcome;
* causal contrast.

Apparent replication failure can be uninterpretable if the target estimand changed.

== Sampling model ==
Suppose study i estimates effect θ:

<pre>
θ̂_i ~ SamplingDistribution(θ_i, SE_i)
</pre>

Exact equality of estimates is not expected.

Replication evidence concerns whether estimates are compatible with a shared or related effect structure.

== Heterogeneity ==
Across replications:

<pre>
θ_i ~ Distribution(μ, τ²)
</pre>

where τ² describes between-study heterogeneity under a random-effects model.

Large heterogeneity can indicate:
* real context dependence;
* methodological differences;
* measurement non-equivalence;
* bias;
* unidentified moderators.

== Significance mismatch ==
A common error is:

<pre>
original p < 0.05
replication p > 0.05
therefore effects differ
</pre>

Different significance outcomes do not prove that the estimated effects differ significantly from each other.

Direct comparison of effect estimates and uncertainty is needed.

== Publication and selection bias ==
Published original effects can be exaggerated because:
* significant results are preferentially published;
* multiple analyses were attempted;
* small studies have high sampling variance;
* flexible stopping/measurement decisions select extreme estimates.

Replication can correct these biases, especially when protocols and analyses are pre-specified.

== Multi-site designs ==
Hierarchical models can separate:
* participant variation;
* site variation;
* intervention variation;
* measurement variation.

This allows replication to estimate not only whether an effect exists, but how stable it is across environments.

== Bayesian updating ==
Replication can update a prior/posterior distribution:

<pre>
P(θ | E_original, E_replication)
∝ P(E_replication | θ) P(θ | E_original)
</pre>

The result depends on model assumptions and independence between evidence sources.

